---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="5.8 Research and Development">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit5/">Unit 5</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>5.8 Research and Development</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">5.8</span>
    <h1>Research and Development <span class="hl-badge">HL Only</span></h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Why R&amp;D matters, types of innovation, and protecting ideas with patents, copyright and trademarks.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Define intellectual property</li>
        <li>Analyse patents, copyright and trademarks</li>
        <li>Evaluate the pros and cons of protecting ideas</li>
        <li>Explain why R&amp;D matters, and incremental vs disruptive innovation</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Why R&amp;D matters</h2>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>This section was written to fill a gap in the lesson slides.</p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Research and development (R&amp;D)</strong> is investigating and testing new products and processes. It keeps a business competitive as products reach the end of their life cycle, and can create a USP. The catch: it's expensive, slow, and many projects fail.</p>
      <p>Good R&amp;D meets <strong>unmet needs</strong>, including ones customers don't know they have yet (nobody asked for a smartphone before one existed).</p>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Incremental innovation</div><div class="definition">Small, continuous improvements to existing products, services or processes. Lower risk, the most common kind.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Disruptive innovation</div><div class="definition">Something so new it creates a new market and disrupts an existing one. Rare, high risk, high reward.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Intellectual property (IP)</h2>
      <p>Creations of the mind: inventions, artistic works, brand names. An <strong>intangible asset</strong> with real value, protected by patents, copyright and trademarks.</p>
      <p><strong>Why protect it?</strong> A USP, market share, valuable assets, and a return on R&amp;D spending. It also encourages innovation: firms can profit from a new idea because they get monopoly rights over using it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Patents, copyright, trademarks</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Patent</th><th>Copyright</th><th>Trademark</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Protects</strong></td><td>An invention: exclusive right to make, use or sell it</td><td>Creative work: books, music, film, art, software</td><td>A symbol, word or phrase linked to a business: names, logos, fonts</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>How you get it</strong></td><td>Approved by a government patent office; must be original and viable</td><td>Automatic, no application</td><td>Usually registered with a government authority</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>How long</strong></td><td>Up to 20 years in many countries</td><td>Many years, even after the creator's death</td><td>As long as it's registered and defended</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Example</strong></td><td>Samsung holds over 200,000 patents</td><td>J.K. Rowling and Harry Potter</td><td>The Apple logo, the Coca-Cola font</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Advantages</strong></td><td>Competitors can't copy; recover R&amp;D costs; temporary monopoly; incentive to innovate</td><td>Free and automatic; long-lasting; royalty income from licensing; stops others profiting</td><td>Builds brand value and loyalty; intangible asset on the balance sheet; barrier to entry; stands out from rivals</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Disadvantages</strong></td><td>Costly and complex to file; expires, then anyone can copy; can block others building on the idea</td><td>Hard to enforce against piracy (piracy cost gaming $51 billion in 2021); protects the work, not the idea; limits reuse</td><td>Registration and legal costs; must be defended; protects the brand, not the product; counterfeits still appear</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Royalties</div>
        <p>Fees paid to the owner for the right to use their work.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Big question</h2>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Is innovation supported or hindered by ownership of knowledge?</div>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://podcasts.apple.com/cy/podcast/can-we-build-a-common-good-economy-with-mariana-mazzucato/id1445901378?i=1000789809473" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mariana Mazzucato</a> argues markets are shaped by rules, institutions and public investment. Is innovation only a private company thing?</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2013/jun/02/aaron-swartz-hacker-genius-martyr-girlfriend-interview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aaron Swartz</a> helped build the modern internet, then was prosecuted for trying to free academic research from paywalls. Hero or lawbreaker?</li>
          <li><a href="https://opensource.stanford.edu/what-is-open-source-why-does-it-matter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source</a> software improves because anyone can see and fix the code. Why give valuable work away?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>R&amp;D keeps products competitive and can meet needs customers don't yet know they have</li>
        <li>Innovation is incremental (small, common) or disruptive (rare, market-changing)</li>
        <li>IP is an intangible asset; protecting it rewards R&amp;D</li>
        <li>Patents protect inventions, copyright protects creative work, trademarks protect the brand</li>
        <li>Every form costs something to get or enforce, and can limit others building on the idea</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Which IP matters most?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Without looking, make a table with three columns (patents, copyright, trademarks): what each protects, how long it lasts, one advantage and one disadvantage. Then choose a real business. Which type of IP matters most to it? Justify in two sentences.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Discuss</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Why might a pharmaceutical firm value patents more than a fashion brand does?</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Hint</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">Drug R&amp;D costs are huge and a copy is cheap to make once the formula is known. Fashion relies more on brand (trademarks) and moves too fast for a long patent process to pay off.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Who benefits when a patent expires?</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Hint</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">Competitors, who can now copy it, and customers, who get cheaper versions (think generic medicines).</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">How can a trademark become a barrier to entry?</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Hint</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">Customers trust and are loyal to the established brand, and a newcomer can't use anything close to it, so it has to spend heavily to build its own recognition.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">To what extent does IP protection help small businesses?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>The hints above are pending teacher review.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: The big question</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Pick one of the three readings in the Notes tab. Is innovation supported or hindered by ownership of knowledge? Argue both sides, then decide.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

